[hermes:err-live] ### pytorch/pytorch#191618 — [symm_mem] check_channel() multiplies by world_size instead of dividing, allowing out-of-bounds signal writes which silently corrupts peer tensor data on main
[hermes:err-live] - State: `OPEN` · Closing-link from PR: yes · Source: `closing_hash`
[hermes:err-live] - URL: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/191618
[hermes:err-live] - Author: RohitRathore1
[hermes:err-live] - Labels: oncall: distributed, triaged, module: correctness (silent), module: symm_mem, bot-triaged, oncall: distributed infra
[hermes:err-live] #### Issue body
[hermes:err-live] ### 🐛 Describe the bug
[hermes:err-live] `check_channel()` computes the channel capacity as:
[hermes:err-live] https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/050ce9e1226b95d6b5938bdd5e245491f7219fd8/torch/csrc/distributed/c10d/symm_mem/CUDASymmetricMemory-inl.cuh#L115-L131
[hermes:err-live] ```cpp
[hermes:err-live] const size_t num_channels =
[hermes:err-live]     signal_pad_size / sizeof(uint32_t) * world_size;
[hermes:err-live] ```
[hermes:err-live] The signal pad holds `signal_pad_size / sizeof(uint32_t)` slots per rank, and each channel consumes `world_size` slots (slot index = `world_size * channel + rank`, see `barrier_kernel` just below in the same file), so the capacity should be:
[hermes:err-live] ```cpp
[hermes:err-live] const size_t num_channels =
[hermes:err-live]     signal_pad_size / sizeof(uint32_t) / world_size;
[hermes:err-live] ```
[hermes:err-live] With the default 9216-byte pad and world_size = 2, the real capacity is 1152 channels, but the check accepts channels up to 4607 -- a factor of world_size^2 too permissive. `check_channel` is shared by the CUDA and NCCL backends, so `barrier()`, `put_signal()` and `wait_signal()` on both accept out-of-range channels.
[hermes:err-live] ## Consequence
[hermes:err-live] Since #189088 (https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/commit/c99d06a01764f62f60e3d1246fb026e63146e026) the signal pad sits at the front of the symmetric allocation, directly before the data buffer, so a signal op on an over-capacity channel writes its `1` past the pad straight into the peer's tensor data, silently.
[hermes:err-live] Verified on 2x H200 (NVLink), main @ 050ce9e1226:
[hermes:err-live] ```
[hermes:err-live] pad=9216B slots=2304 real_capacity=1152 channel=2000 -> writes 6784B past the pad
[hermes:err-live] put_signal(channel=2000) was ACCEPTED (check_channel passed)
[hermes:err-live] [rank 1] DATA CORRUPTED: t[1696] bits=0x00000001 (predicted victim element 1696)
[hermes:err-live] ```
[hermes:err-live] The corrupted element is exactly where the slot arithmetic predicts. A correct bounds check would have raised instead.
[hermes:err-live] ## Repro
[hermes:err-live] ```python
[hermes:err-live] # torchrun --standalone --nproc-per-node=2 repro.py
[hermes:err-live] import os, torch, torch.distributed as dist
[hermes:err-live] import torch.distributed._symmetric_memory as symm_mem
[hermes:err-live] rank = int(os.environ["RANK"])
[hermes:err-live] torch.cuda.set_device(rank)
[hermes:err-live] dist.init_process_group("nccl")
[hermes:err-live] t = symm_mem.empty(4096, dtype=torch.float32, device=f"cuda:{rank}")
[hermes:err-live] t.zero_()
[hermes:err-live] hdl = symm_mem.rendezvous(t, dist.group.WORLD)
[hermes:err-live] dist.barrier()
[hermes:err-live] if rank == 0:
[hermes:err-live]     # capacity with default pad and ws=2 is 1152; 2000 passes the broken check
[hermes:err-live]     hdl.put_signal(dst_rank=1, channel=2000)
[hermes:err-live] torch.cuda.synchronize()
[hermes:err-live] dist.barrier()
[hermes:err-live] if rank == 1:
[hermes:err-live]     print("nonzero elements:", t.nonzero().flatten())  # expected: none
[hermes:err-live] ```
[hermes:err-live] ## History / affected versions
[hermes:err-live] The incorrect bound is unchanged since the original SymmetricMemory prototype (#128582, June 2024):
[hermes:err-live] https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/8771e3429c3d7327f08c48d547ad73546d5603b3/torch/csrc/distributed/c10d/CUDASymmetricMemory.cu#L214
[hermes:err-live] so every release with symmetric memory accepts out-of-range channels. The *consequence* differs by layout:
[hermes:err-live] - Released versions use the old `[data buffer | signal pad]` layout, where the overflow lands past the end of the allocation -- in the granularity-rounded tail padding for moderate channels, or past the mapped VA for large ones.
[hermes:err-live] - On main, after #189088 moved the pad to the front (`[signal pad | data buffer]`), the overflow lands in the peer's tensor data. Unless fixed, 2.14 will be the first release where an over-capacity channel silently corrupts user data.
[hermes:err-live] ## Fix
[hermes:err-live] I can send a PR with the fix plus a test asserting that an over-capacity channel raises on both the CUDA and NCCL backends.
